Indigo Buntings, like many migratory birds, fly thousands of miles during their seasonal migrations. Unlucky ones may get killed in human communities along the way.  But there are ways for people to help them survive. 
Every year billions of birds die from glass window collisions, or from house cat attacks. These incidents can be avoided, and injured birds can be taken care of.&nbsp;
"Sometimes you can actually see the bird’s outline, feathers and all,” says Robert Mulvihill, an ornithologist for the National Aviary, while walking the streets of Pittsburgh looking for fall migrants that had collided with buildings the night before. "The reflection is pretty bad here," he says, as the morning traffic creates a wall of noise behind us. "You can see the trees reflected in the glass? The birds…

